  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "The loss is (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half its value as a surface with contour lines. It is 3 times steeper along x, so overshooting shows up there first. Steps diverge along x when the learning rate is above 2/3.",
    "Each step moves by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, with standard deviation 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b, so it is exactly 0 at 64.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at 3 times the step they stand for (the same scale for both). The orange arrow is the mini-batch step the ball takes next. The grey arrow is −learning rate × the true slope.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or as diverged once the loss goes above 6, which is the loss at the bowl's edge along x. A diverged ball is drawn held at the bowl's rim, and the readout still shows the true loss.",
    "Noise values come from a fixed seed and continue in order across new runs. Reset replays the sequence from the start. Running takes 5 steps per second at speed 1."
  ]
}